    <!-- Pest advice -->
    <string name="pest_advice_ants">Apply diatomaceous earth around the plant base. Use bait traps and remove plant debris. Keep the soil surface clean to discourage nesting.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_bees">Bees are essential pollinators — do not kill them. If a hive is too close, contact a local beekeeper in your area for safe relocation.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_beetle">Hand-pick beetles in early morning when they are sluggish. Apply neem oil spray weekly. Use row covers on young transplants to prevent access.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_caterpillar">Apply Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) spray, which is organic and widely available in Pakistan. Hand-pick visible caterpillars. Encourage parasitic wasps by planting dill or fennel nearby.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_earthworms">Earthworms are highly beneficial — they aerate soil and improve fertility. No action needed. Their presence indicates healthy soil.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_earwig">Set traps using damp rolled newspaper placed near plants overnight. Remove garden debris and mulch piles where earwigs hide during the day.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_grasshopper">Use floating row covers to protect crops. Apply neem oil or Nosema locustae bait for biological control. Remove weeds around field borders where they breed.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_moth">Use pheromone traps to monitor and reduce moth populations. Apply Bt spray to control larvae. Inspect leaf undersides regularly and remove egg clusters.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_slug">Set beer traps (shallow containers filled with beer) near affected plants. Apply iron phosphate bait pellets. Create copper tape barriers around pots or raised beds.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_snail">Hand-pick snails during early morning or after rain. Use iron phosphate bait. Apply copper tape around stems and containers to create an electric barrier.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_wasp">Wasps are beneficial predators that eat caterpillars and aphids. Avoid disturbing nests unless they are dangerously close to work areas. Relocate if necessary.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_weevil">Apply neem oil to foliage. Use sticky traps around plant stems. Remove and destroy heavily infested plant parts. Rotate crops to break the lifecycle.</string>
    <string name="pest_advice_unknown">Unable to determine pest type. Please retake the photo with clearer lighting and ensure the pest is fully visible in the frame.</string>

    <!-- =========================
         Ripeness Metadata
         ========================= -->
    <string name="ripeness_advice_ripe">Harvest immediately and store at room temperature, stem-side down, out of direct sunlight. Do not refrigerate — cold temperatures destroy flavor and cause a mealy texture. For Pakistani markets, harvest in early morning when the fruit is cool. Use within 3–5 days for best quality.</string>
    <string name="ripeness_advice_unripe">Leave the tomato on the vine for 5–10 more days until it shows full color. If temperatures exceed 35°C, harvest at the breaker stage (first hint of pink) and ripen indoors at 20–25°C. Avoid direct sunlight during indoor ripening.</string>
    <string name="ripeness_advice_unknown">Unable to determine ripeness stage. Ensure the photo clearly shows the tomato surface with natural lighting. Try cropping the image to focus only on the fruit, removing any background leaves or soil.</string>

